{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790295652,"nonce":"0HKdXNMTMFMul7PVaOFmNo42+4w=","message":"Update from 64ccb002acd02e6a7ac073a994a3d5572a74b660:\n- Tombstones of dead weak keys are no longer compacted by collectgarbage. A table holding them is compacted when a new key is next stored into its hash part (any collection, automatic ones included), so a collection during a pairs/next traversal keeps every entry's position. A weak-key cache that never calls collectgarbage now stays bounded: 40 x 50000 fresh keys, NUC peak RSS 424 MB before, 121 MB after (lua5.4 2.4 MB; the rest is the heap budget, not tombstones).\n- collectgarbage('step') still runs a full collection, but now returns true only in incremental mode; in generational mode it returns false, matching 5.4.7's generational step, which never ends at the pause state.\n- Correction: coroutines do NOT leave weak tables unless they have finished. A suspended or never-started coroutine is rooted by the fiber runtime (its stack and env are roots) and is never collected; filed as 224cd147eb57b76b29c83e088dcf50fcc0481481b9f2fb16561622d3751f1166.","files":null}]}